1. Install BIND9 DNS server on your Linux machine and verify the installation by checking the version with the named -v command.
=>apt-get install -y bind9 bind9utils dnsutils
named -v
Output:
textBIND 9.18.39-0ubuntu0.24.04.7-Ubuntu (Extended Support Version)

2. Configure a basic DNS zone file in /etc/bind/ for a custom domain like myzomatoclone.local, mapping it to 127.0.0.1, and restart the DNS service to apply changes.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Edit named.conf.local and create a new zone file using db.local as a template.</em>
=>Zone file /etc/bind/db.myzomatoclone.local:
text$TTL    604800
@       IN      SOA     ns.myzomatoclone.local. admin.myzomatoclone.local. (
                              2         ; Serial
                         604800         ; Refresh
                          86400         ; Retry
                        2419200         ; Expire
                         604800 )       ; Negative Cache TTL
;
@       IN      NS      ns.myzomatoclone.local.
@       IN      A       127.0.0.1
ns      IN      A       127.0.0.1
foodsearch IN  CNAME   myzomatoclone.local.
Added to /etc/bind/named.conf.local:
textzone "myzomatoclone.local" {
    type master;
    file "/etc/bind/db.myzomatoclone.local";
};
Validation:
Bashnamed-checkconf
named-checkzone myzomatoclone.local /etc/bind/db.myzomatoclone.local
→ OK
Restarted service:
Bashservice named start

3. Set your system's DNS resolver to use your local BIND9 server and test domain resolution for myzomatoclone.local using the dig or nslookup command.
=>echo "nameserver 127.0.0.1" > /etc/resolv.conf
dig @127.0.0.1 myzomatoclone.local +short
nslookup myzomatoclone.local 127.0.0.1
Result:
text127.0.0.1
Name:	myzomatoclone.local
Address: 127.0.0.1

4. Add a CNAME record in your zone file to create an alias foodsearch.myzomatoclone.local that points to myzomatoclone.local, then verify the alias works with dig.
=>
foodsearch IN  CNAME   myzomatoclone.local.
Verification:
Bashdig @127.0.0.1 foodsearch.myzomatoclone.local +short
textmyzomatoclone.local.
127.0.0.1
5. Find and fix the error in this zone file snippet: 'myzomatoclone.local. IN A 127.0.0.1 foodsearch IN CNAME myzomatoclone.local.' — the CNAME is not resolving as expected.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Check the syntax and placement of the CNAME record.</em>
=>myzomatoclone.local. IN A 127.0.0.1 foodsearch IN CNAME myzomatoclone.local.
Problems:

Two records are written on a single line without a newline — BIND treats this as invalid syntax.
The CNAME owner name foodsearch is missing the trailing dot or proper relative context in this jammed format.
A CNAME should not appear on the same line as an A record.

Correct syntax (separate lines):
textmyzomatoclone.local.    IN    A       127.0.0.1
foodsearch              IN    CNAME   myzomatoclone.local.
or fully qualified:
textfoodsearch.myzomatoclone.local.  IN  CNAME  myzomatoclone.local.
